.TH COMPRESS 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compress, zcat \- compress files with LZW, or write expanded data to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compress
.RB [ \-cfqv ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR bits ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B zcat
.RB [ \-fv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compress
reduces the size of each
.I file
using adaptive Lempel-Ziv-Welch coding and writes the result to
.IR file .Z
in the format used by historic Unix
.B compress
(magic bytes 0x1f 0x9d, block mode enabled).
On success the original
.I file
is removed and its permission bits are copied to the new file.
Ownership and timestamps are not copied.
.PP
A
.I file
whose name already ends in
.B .Z
is skipped with a diagnostic.
If
.IR file .Z
already exists it is not overwritten unless
.B \-f
is given.
Unless
.B \-f
is given, if the compressed file is not smaller than the original, it
is removed, the original is kept, and a
.B "file would grow; not compressed"
message is printed.
.PP
If no
.I file
is given, or
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
standard input is compressed to standard output.
.PP
.B zcat
is a link to
.BR compress .
When invoked under that name it runs
.B uncompress \-c
with the remaining arguments, looking
.B uncompress
up in
.BR PATH ,
so each
.I file
is expanded to standard output and left in place.
The options it accepts are those of
.BR uncompress (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b " bits"
Limit codes to at most
.I bits
bits.
.I bits
must be between 9 and 16; the default is 16.
.TP
.B \-c
Write the compressed data to standard output and leave the original
.I file
in place.
The size check described above is not applied.
.TP
.B \-f
Overwrite an existing
.IR file .Z,
and keep the compressed result even if it is not smaller than the
original.
.TP
.B \-q
Do not print the
.B "file would grow"
message.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the compression ratio of each file, and the message
.IB file ": compressed"
after a file is replaced, to standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or
.B \-b
value was given, or at least one file could not be read, written or
removed, already had a
.B .Z
suffix, had an existing output file, or would have grown.
Remaining files are still processed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Searched by
.B zcat
for the
.B uncompress
program.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compress a file, replacing it with
.IR notes.txt.Z :
.PP
.EX
$ compress notes.txt
.EE
.PP
Display a compressed file:
.PP
.EX
$ zcat notes.txt.Z
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uncompress (1),
.BR pax (1),
.BR tar (1)
.SH BUGS
Unlike traditional implementations,
.B compress
exits with status 1, not 2, when a file would grow.
.PP
Neither
.B compress
nor
.B zcat
refuses to write compressed data to a terminal.
.PP
File names are limited to 1023 bytes.
